{"global_index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10720", "question": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Find the Laplace transform of f(t) = t^2.\n\nOptions: \nA. (2 / s^4)\nB. (1 / s^3)\nC. (t^2 / s)\nD. (t^2 / s^3)\nE. (2 / s^3)\nF. (4 / s^3)\nG. (3 / s^4)\nH. (2 / s^2)\nI. (3 / s^3)\nJ. (1 / s^2)\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "llm_selected": "gpt-4o-mini", "generated_answer": "The Laplace transform of \\( f(t) = t^n \\) is given by \\( \\frac{n!}{s^{n+1}} \\). For \\( f(t) = t^2 \\), here \\( n = 2 \\), so we calculate \\( \\frac{2!}{s^{2+1}} = \\frac{2}{s^3} \\). Therefore, the correct answer is \\(\\boxed{E}\\).", "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 171, "output_tokens": 93, "total_tokens": 264}, "success": true, "provider": "openai", "error": null, "evaluation_result": {"extracted_answer": "The Laplace transform of \\( f(t) = t^n \\) is given by \\( \\frac{n!}{s^{n+1}} \\). For \\( f(t) = t^2 \\), here \\( n = 2 \\), so we calculate \\( \\frac{2!}{s^{2+1}} = \\frac{2}{s^3} \\). Therefore, the correct answer is \\(\\boxed{E}\\).", "ground_truth": "4", "score": 1.0, "metric": "mcq_accuracy", "inference_cost": 8.145e-05}}
{"global_index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10821", "question": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Five capacitors each of 5 µF are connected in series, the equivalent capacitance of the system will be\n\nOptions: \nA. 15 µF\nB. 10 µF\nC. 5 µF\nD. 0.1 µF\nE. 2 µF\nF. 0.5 µF\nG. 30 µF\nH. 1 µF\nI. 25 µF\nJ. 20 µF\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "llm_selected": "gpt-4o-mini", "generated_answer": "When capacitors are connected in series, the equivalent capacitance \\( C_{\\text{eq}} \\) can be calculated using the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{C_{\\text{eq}}} = \\frac{1}{C_1} + \\frac{1}{C_2} + \\frac{1}{C_3} + \\ldots\n\\]\n\nFor five capacitors of 5 µF each in series:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{C_{\\text{eq}}} = \\frac{1}{5} + \\frac{1}{5} + \\frac{1}{5} + \\frac{1}{5} + \\frac{1}{5} = \\frac{5}{5} = 1\n\\]\n\nThus, \\( C_{\\text{eq}} = 1 \\, \\mu F \\). The correct answer is \\(\\boxed{H}\\).", "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 163, "output_tokens": 190, "total_tokens": 353}, "success": true, "provider": "openai", "error": null, "evaluation_result": {"extracted_answer": "When capacitors are connected in series, the equivalent capacitance \\( C_{\\text{eq}} \\) can be calculated using the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{C_{\\text{eq}}} = \\frac{1}{C_1} + \\frac{1}{C_2} + \\frac{1}{C_3} + \\ldots\n\\]\n\nFor five capacitors of 5 µF each in series:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{C_{\\text{eq}}} = \\frac{1}{5} + \\frac{1}{5} + \\frac{1}{5} + \\frac{1}{5} + \\frac{1}{5} = \\frac{5}{5} = 1\n\\]\n\nThus, \\( C_{\\text{eq}} = 1 \\, \\mu F \\). The correct answer is \\(\\boxed{H}\\).", "ground_truth": "7", "score": 1.0, "metric": "mcq_accuracy", "inference_cost": 0.00013845}}
{"global_index": "MMLUPro_engineering_9981", "question": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What will be the number of lamps, each having 300 lumens, required to obtain an average illuminance of 50 lux on a 4m * 3m rectangular room?\n\nOptions: \nA. 6\nB. 1\nC. 2\nD. 5\nE. 7\nF. 3\nG. 9\nH. 10\nI. 8\nJ. 4\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "llm_selected": "gpt-4o-mini", "generated_answer": "To determine the number of lamps required, we first calculate the total illuminance needed for the room. The area of the room is \\(4m \\times 3m = 12m^2\\). To achieve an average of 50 lux, we need \\(50 \\, \\text{lux} \\times 12 \\, \\text{m}^2 = 600 \\, \\text{lumens}\\). Since each lamp provides 300 lumens, the number of lamps required is \\(600 \\div 300 = 2\\).\n\nThus, the correct answer is \\(\\boxed{C}\\).", "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 153, "output_tokens": 126, "total_tokens": 279}, "success": true, "provider": "openai", "error": null, "evaluation_result": {"extracted_answer": "To determine the number of lamps required, we first calculate the total illuminance needed for the room. The area of the room is \\(4m \\times 3m = 12m^2\\). To achieve an average of 50 lux, we need \\(50 \\, \\text{lux} \\times 12 \\, \\text{m}^2 = 600 \\, \\text{lumens}\\). Since each lamp provides 300 lumens, the number of lamps required is \\(600 \\div 300 = 2\\).\n\nThus, the correct answer is \\(\\boxed{C}\\).", "ground_truth": "2", "score": 1.0, "metric": "mcq_accuracy", "inference_cost": 9.855e-05}}
{"global_index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10225", "question": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Using the definition of the partial derivative of the vector, find: 1)(\\partiala/ \\partialu) 2)(\\partiala/ \\partialv) 3)[(\\partial^2a) / (\\partialu\\partialv) where a= (u^2v, u +e^v, u + v^2)(1)\n\nOptions: \nA. (2uv, 1, 1), (u^2,e^v, 2v), (2u, 1, 0)\nB. (2u, e^v, 2v), (2uv, 0, 1), (0, 1, 2v)\nC. (2uv, 1, 1), (u^2,e^v, 2u), (2u, 0, 0)\nD. (uv, 1, 2u), (2uv, 0, e^v), (2, e^v, 0)\nE. (2u, 1, 2v), (uv, e^v, 1), (0, 0, 2)\nF. (2uv, 1, 0), (u^2,e^v, 2v), (2u, 0, 0)\nG. (u^2, 0, 1), (2uv, 1, e^v), (0, 2u, 0)\nH. (2uv, 1, 1), (u^2,e^v, 2v), (2u, 0, 0)\nI. (0, 1, 2v), (u^2, 1, 1), (2uv, e^v, 0)\nJ. (2uv, 0, 1), (u^2, 1, 2u), (2, 0, e^v)\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "llm_selected": "gpt-4o-mini", "generated_answer": "To find the partial derivatives of the vector \\( a = (u^2v, u + e^v, u + v^2) \\):\n\n1) For \\( \\frac{\\partial a}{\\partial u} \\), differentiate each component with respect to \\( u \\): \\( (2uv, 1, 1) \\).\n2) For \\( \\frac{\\partial a}{\\partial v} \\), differentiate each component with respect to \\( v \\): \\( (u^2, e^v, 2v) \\).\n3) For the mixed partial derivative \\( \\frac{\\partial^2 a}{\\partial u \\partial v} \\): \\( (2u, 0, 0) \\).\n\nThe option that matches these results is H: \\( (2uv, 1, 1), (u^2, e^v, 2v), (2u, 0, 0) \\).\n\nThus, the correct answer is \\(\\boxed{H}\\).", "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 477, "output_tokens": 208, "total_tokens": 685}, "success": true, "provider": "openai", "error": null, "evaluation_result": {"extracted_answer": "To find the partial derivatives of the vector \\( a = (u^2v, u + e^v, u + v^2) \\):\n\n1) For \\( \\frac{\\partial a}{\\partial u} \\), differentiate each component with respect to \\( u \\): \\( (2uv, 1, 1) \\).\n2) For \\( \\frac{\\partial a}{\\partial v} \\), differentiate each component with respect to \\( v \\): \\( (u^2, e^v, 2v) \\).\n3) For the mixed partial derivative \\( \\frac{\\partial^2 a}{\\partial u \\partial v} \\): \\( (2u, 0, 0) \\).\n\nThe option that matches these results is H: \\( (2uv, 1, 1), (u^2, e^v, 2v), (2u, 0, 0) \\).\n\nThus, the correct answer is \\(\\boxed{H}\\).", "ground_truth": "7", "score": 1.0, "metric": "mcq_accuracy", "inference_cost": 0.00019634999999999998}}
{"global_index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10467", "question": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A six-pole generator requires 4.5 amp shunt-field excitation to give rated voltage at no load, and 7.0 amp to give the same voltage at rated load of 200 amp. There are 750 shunt-field turns per pole. (a) How many series turns must be added to give flat-compound operation, using the short-shunt connection? (b) If 12 turns per pole are added, having a resistance of 0.005 ohm per pole, what should be the resistance of a shunt across the series- field terminals to give the desired flat-compound effect?\n\nOptions: \nA. 8.4, 0.095 ohm\nB. 8.4, 0.107 ohm\nC. 11.0, 0.110 ohm\nD. 9.4, 0.117 ohm\nE. 10.4, 0.107 ohm\nF. 9.0, 0.115 ohm\nG. 8.5, 0.100 ohm\nH. 9.4, 0.107 ohm\nI. 7.5, 0.120 ohm\nJ. 10.4, 0.125 ohm\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "llm_selected": "gpt-4o-mini", "generated_answer": "To achieve flat-compound operation, the required series turns can be calculated based on the difference in shunt-field excitation at no load and full load conditions. Once the necessary series turns are established and the additional resistance of the series field is determined, the resistance of the shunt needed for the desired adjustment can be calculated accordingly.\n\nAfter calculations, the most accurate combination of series turns and shunt resistance values corresponds to the option: \\(\\boxed{H}\\).", "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 331, "output_tokens": 92, "total_tokens": 423}, "success": true, "provider": "openai", "error": null, "evaluation_result": {"extracted_answer": "To achieve flat-compound operation, the required series turns can be calculated based on the difference in shunt-field excitation at no load and full load conditions. Once the necessary series turns are established and the additional resistance of the series field is determined, the resistance of the shunt needed for the desired adjustment can be calculated accordingly.\n\nAfter calculations, the most accurate combination of series turns and shunt resistance values corresponds to the option: \\(\\boxed{H}\\).", "ground_truth": "7", "score": 1.0, "metric": "mcq_accuracy", "inference_cost": 0.00010485}}
{"global_index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10915", "question": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Calculate the electrostatic energy of a system consisting of a point charge q located at the center of a spherical conducting shell of inner radius r_1 and outer radius r_2.\n\nOptions: \nA. {{1 / (4\\pi\\epsilon_0)}} (q / r^2)\nB. {{1 / (4\\pi\\epsilon_0)}} (1/4) q^2 {{(1 / r_1) - (1 / r_2)}}\nC. {{1 / (4\\pi\\epsilon_0)}} (q / r_1) - (q / r_2)\nD. {{1 / (4\\pi\\epsilon_0)}} (1/2) q^2 {{(1 / r_1) - (1 / r_2)}}\nE. {{1 / (4\\pi\\epsilon_0)}} (1/2) q^2 {{(1 / r^2) - (1 / r_1)}}\nF. {{1 / (4\\pi\\epsilon_0)}} q^2 {{(r_2 - r_1) / (r_1 r_2)}}\nG. {{1 / (4\\pi\\epsilon_0)}} (q^2 / r_1)\nH. {{1 / (4\\pi\\epsilon_0)}} (q^2 / r_2)\nI. {{1 / (4\\pi\\epsilon_0)}} (q^2 / r_1) - (q^2 / r_2)\nJ. {{1 / (4\\pi\\epsilon_0)}} (1/2) q^2 {{(1 / r_1) + (1 / r_2)}}\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "llm_selected": "gpt-4o-mini", "generated_answer": "The correct answer is based on the formula for the electrostatic energy of a point charge inside a conducting shell, which is typically given by \\(U = \\frac{1}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0} \\frac{1}{2} q^2 \\left( \\frac{1}{r_1} - \\frac{1}{r_2} \\right)\\). Thus, the most likely correct option is:\n\n\\(\\boxed{D}\\)", "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 423, "output_tokens": 95, "total_tokens": 518}, "success": true, "provider": "openai", "error": null, "evaluation_result": {"extracted_answer": "The correct answer is based on the formula for the electrostatic energy of a point charge inside a conducting shell, which is typically given by \\(U = \\frac{1}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0} \\frac{1}{2} q^2 \\left( \\frac{1}{r_1} - \\frac{1}{r_2} \\right)\\). Thus, the most likely correct option is:\n\n\\(\\boxed{D}\\)", "ground_truth": "4", "score": 0.0, "metric": "mcq_accuracy", "inference_cost": 0.00012045}}
{"global_index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10316", "question": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Design a bar to resist pure tension of 4000 lb., based on a factor of safety of 2.5. Tensile strength of the material = 175 psi.\n\nOptions: \nA. 14 in. × 3 in.\nB. 16 in. × 4 in.\nC. 18 in. × 6 in.\nD. 15 in. × 5 in.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "llm_selected": "gpt-4o-mini", "generated_answer": "First, calculate the required tensile strength by considering the factor of safety: \\( \\text{Required strength} = \\frac{4000 \\text{ lb}}{2.5} = 1600 \\text{ lb} \\). Next, use the tensile strength of the material, 175 psi, to determine the area needed: \\( A = \\frac{1600 \\text{ lb}}{175 \\text{ psi}} \\approx 9.14 \\text{ in}^2 \\). Among the options listed, the dimension that yields the area closest to or greater than 9.14 in² is option A (14 in. × 3 in.), which provides an area of 42 in², thus the most appropriate choice is \\(\\boxed{A}\\).", "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 145, "output_tokens": 160, "total_tokens": 305}, "success": true, "provider": "openai", "error": null, "evaluation_result": {"extracted_answer": "First, calculate the required tensile strength by considering the factor of safety: \\( \\text{Required strength} = \\frac{4000 \\text{ lb}}{2.5} = 1600 \\text{ lb} \\). Next, use the tensile strength of the material, 175 psi, to determine the area needed: \\( A = \\frac{1600 \\text{ lb}}{175 \\text{ psi}} \\approx 9.14 \\text{ in}^2 \\). Among the options listed, the dimension that yields the area closest to or greater than 9.14 in² is option A (14 in. × 3 in.), which provides an area of 42 in², thus the most appropriate choice is \\(\\boxed{A}\\).", "ground_truth": "1", "score": 0.0, "metric": "mcq_accuracy", "inference_cost": 0.00011775}}
{"global_index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10642", "question": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The total normalized power of a periodic signal is given by S = ^n=+\\infty\\sum_n=-\\inftyV_nV_n\\textasteriskcentered where V_n= [1 / (\\vertn\\vert)]e-jarctan(n/2)forn = \\pm 1, \\pm 2,... Calculate the ratio: [{{normalized power(second harmonic)}} / {{normalized power (first harmonic)}}].\n\nOptions: \nA. 0.125\nB. 1.5\nC. 0.75\nD. 0.5\nE. 2\nF. 4\nG. 0.6\nH. 1\nI. 0.25\nJ. 3\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "llm_selected": "gpt-4o-mini", "generated_answer": "To find the ratio of normalized power of the second harmonic to the first harmonic, we calculate the values of \\( V_n \\) specifically for \\( n = 1 \\) (first harmonic) and \\( n = 2 \\) (second harmonic). Plugging these values into the formula \\( V_n = \\frac{1}{|n|} e^{-j \\arctan(n/2)} \\) gives us \\( V_1 = \\frac{1}{1} e^{-j \\arctan(1/2)} \\) and \\( V_2 = \\frac{1}{2} e^{-j \\arctan(2/2)} \\), leading to the normalized power ratio of \\( \\frac{1^2}{(1/2)^2} = 4 \\). Thus, the correct choice is \\(\\boxed{F}\\).", "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 218, "output_tokens": 179, "total_tokens": 397}, "success": true, "provider": "openai", "error": null, "evaluation_result": {"extracted_answer": "To find the ratio of normalized power of the second harmonic to the first harmonic, we calculate the values of \\( V_n \\) specifically for \\( n = 1 \\) (first harmonic) and \\( n = 2 \\) (second harmonic). Plugging these values into the formula \\( V_n = \\frac{1}{|n|} e^{-j \\arctan(n/2)} \\) gives us \\( V_1 = \\frac{1}{1} e^{-j \\arctan(1/2)} \\) and \\( V_2 = \\frac{1}{2} e^{-j \\arctan(2/2)} \\), leading to the normalized power ratio of \\( \\frac{1^2}{(1/2)^2} = 4 \\). Thus, the correct choice is \\(\\boxed{F}\\).", "ground_truth": "8", "score": 0.0, "metric": "mcq_accuracy", "inference_cost": 0.0001401}}
{"global_index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10094", "question": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Describe the charges required to obtain the field D= {{r + (1 / r^2)}}U (r_0 - r)1_r\n\nOptions: \nA. No uniform charge density, a surface charge of - [r_0 + (1/r_0)] on the sphere of radius r = r_0, and an additional point charge of 4π coulombs at the origin.\nB. A uniform charge density of 2 C/m^3, a surface charge of - [r_0 + (1/r_0^2)] on the sphere of radius r = r_0, and an additional point charge of 2π coulombs at the origin.\nC. A uniform charge density of 4 C/m^3, no surface charge on the sphere, and an additional point charge of 4π coulombs at the origin.\nD. A uniform charge density of 3 C/m^3, a surface charge of - [2r_0 + (1/r_0^2)] on the sphere of radius r = r_0, and an additional point charge of π coulombs at the origin.\nE. A uniform charge density of 3 C/m^3, a surface charge of - [r_0 + (1/r_0^2)] on the sphere of radius r = r_0, and an additional point charge of 2π coulombs at the origin.\nF. A uniform charge density of 2.5 C/m^3, a surface charge of - [r_0 + (1/r_0)] on the sphere of radius r = r_0, and an additional point charge of 5π coulombs at the origin.\nG. A uniform charge density of 2 C/m^3, a surface charge of - [r_0 + (1/r_0^2)] on the sphere of radius r = r_0, and an additional point charge of 4π coulombs at the origin.\nH. A uniform charge density of 3 C/m^3, a surface charge of - [r_0 + (1/r_0^3)] on the sphere of radius r = r_0, and an additional point charge of 2π coulombs at the origin.\nI. A uniform charge density of 1 C/m^3, a surface charge of - [r_0 + (2/r_0^2)] on the sphere of radius r = r_0, and an additional point charge of 3π coulombs at the origin.\nJ. A uniform charge density of 3 C/m^3, a surface charge of - [r_0 + (1/r_0^2)] on the sphere of radius r = r_0, and an additional point charge of 4π coulombs at the 